 "conclusions": [
  "Pulse is recurring hubs, not dated pages. The two biggest Pulse pages found anywhere are this weekend hubs, one URL per city refreshed continuously. Fever's largest page is an event series by city. Dated pages earn while the date is near and then earn nothing, which is an argument for a lifecycle rather than for a page per date.",
  "Places earn at two levels and the narrower one can win. Restaurants in Soho takes more traffic than best spas in London, so the neighbourhood level page is not a weaker version of the city level page. Both are worth having where the neighbourhood is itself searched.",
  "Individual POI and event pages earn only when the thing has a name people search. Angels Landing at 82,000 a month earns a page; a nameless trail does not. Fever's Arte Museum Las Vegas at 34,000 earns a page; a dated concert does not. This is one rule with two applications, and it is the gate.",
  "Community indexes the hub, not the group. Meetup's top pages are city by topic hubs, and individual groups are absent. That settles how Livdar should treat user generated meetups.",
  "Coworking has a low ceiling in English and a high one in local languages. Coworker.com's best city hub takes 170, while coworking barcelona alone is 2,100 searches a month at a three dollar cost per click.",
  "A famous product can have no search surface. Nomads.com city profiles take between 10 and 47 visits each. Livdar should not build that shape at scale."
 ]
